      contexto: 'Pluna, the flag carrier, stopped flying in July 2012 under José Mujica\'s government. To pay debts, the state auctioned seven of the airline\'s aircraft that year. A single bidder appeared: Cosmo, a purported European airline represented by Hernán Antonio Calvo Sánchez — who went by "Antonio Sánchez" — whom La Nación describes as the Buquebus group\'s representative in Spain. To enable that bid, Banco de la República issued a guarantee. According to La Nación, the "buyer" paid neither the US$137 million promised for the aircraft nor the US$13.7 million guarantee, and stated it would neither hire Pluna\'s former workers nor open an airline in Uruguay; creditors left behind by the law that enabled the auction challenged it before the Supreme Court and the judiciary declared it unconstitutional.',
      hallazgo: 'In April 2014 former economy minister Fernando Lorenzo and Banco República president Fernando Calloia were charged without detention with abuse of office over the irregular guarantee granted to Cosmo, worth US$13.7 million within a US$137 million transaction, according to La Nación. On 18 December 2017 judge María Helena Mainard resolved the case: she acquitted Lorenzo and convicted Calloia. Subrayado and El Observador reported that same day that the sentence was 20 months in prison, a two-year special disqualification and a fine of 1,000 adjustable units — around one million pesos — and that Calloia would not serve prison time because the offence carries release. El Observador noted Lorenzo was acquitted because, per the ruling, "it is not proven that he acted as verbal guarantor of the operation", and that his ministry\'s communication asking BROU to work on the guarantee did not amount to a formal guarantee.',
      statusNote: 'The ruling was handed down on 18 December 2017: conviction of Fernando Calloia for abuse of office and acquittal of Fernando Lorenzo. None of the sources consulted carries Calloia\'s or his defence\'s response to the conviction. As of this file\'s closing (August 2026), those sources also show no developments after December 2017 on appeals or cassation in this case, so it cannot be stated here that the conviction is final or that it was overturned.',
      porQueImporta: 'Pluna\'s closure and the failed auction left aircraft grounded, debts growing and a state guarantee on the line. It is the case that shows the cost of deciding quickly about public assets without checking who is on the other side of the counter: what went uncollected and what was litigated afterwards was ultimately absorbed by the state.',
      caveat: 'Neither the auction nor the bank guarantee is a public purchase: they leave no trace in the open procurement data. The cross-reference can only show routine purchases by the bodies involved, not the transaction that triggered the criminal case.',
