      contexto: 'Beyond the domestic criminal case, Pluna\'s 2012 closure opened an international front. Latin American Regional Aviation Company Holding (Larah), a Panamanian firm that had acquired shares in the airline\'s parent company, sued Uruguay before the World Bank\'s International Centre for Settlement of Investment Disputes (ICSID), invoking the bilateral investment protection treaty with Panama. Published figures for the claim differ: El Observador reported that its initial claim sought around US$800 million, while La Nación put it at US$860 million. In February 2024 the tribunal ruled against the Uruguayan state for expropriation and breach of the fair and equitable treatment obligation.',
      hallazgo: 'La Nación reported on 14 February 2024 that ICSID ordered Uruguay to pay US$30 million plus interest, with a penalty of US$500,000 for each month of delay, and that the tribunal found the government "economically asphyxiated Pluna to avoid the contingencies arising from Varig\'s bankruptcy": it obstructed a loan when the airline was in serious illiquidity, officials made damaging public statements, the state oil company ANCAP pressed for immediate fuel payment, and the successor airline was granted tax benefits previously denied to private investors. El Observador reported on 30 January 2025 that the state agreed to pay the US$30 million plus interest, after talks held since May 2024 that broke down on 14 November that year, and that the government did not accept the criminal immunity Larah sought for those accused of fraud in Pluna\'s management.',
      statusNote: 'la diaria reported on 8 September 2025 — citing El País — that Uruguayan state accounts in Luxembourg were under embargo from 22 November to 27 August over non-payment of the award, and that payment was made on 30 May. The presidential pro-secretary, Jorge Díaz, said it "bothered him quite a lot" that the previous government had not reported the embargo during the transition. As of this file\'s closing (August 2026) there are no developments after September 2025.',
      porQueImporta: 'The award\'s US$30 million, plus interest and the monthly penalty accrued while it went unpaid, came out of general revenue: money that was not chosen for a service but had to be paid over a decision taken more than a decade earlier. The embargo episode also shows that the bill for international litigation can arrive without warning.',
      caveat: 'Paying an arbitral award is not a public purchase and does not appear in the open procurement data. Contracts for legal advice and international representation tied to the state\'s defence may appear, but they are only part of the cost.',
